.TH GPT 8
.SH NAME
gpt \- inspect and edit a GUID Partition Table
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gpt -p
.I device
.br
.B gpt -c
.I device
.br
.B gpt -r
.I device
.br
.B gpt -m
[
.B -n
]
.I device
.br
.B gpt -a
[
.B -b
.I first-sector
]
[
.B -s
.I sectors
]
[
.B -t
.I type-guid
]
[
.B -l
.I label
]
.I device
[
.I partition
]
.br
.B gpt -d
.I device partition
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpt
operates on a UEFI GUID Partition Table using 512-byte logical sectors and
64-bit LBAs. It validates the protective MBR, header CRC32, complete 128-entry
array CRC32, usable ranges, unique GUID presence, and partition overlap.
.PP
The print operation is read-only. It validates both copies and reports an
invalid primary, invalid backup, or two individually valid copies which do
not agree. If the primary is invalid but the backup is valid, it prints the
backup without modifying the disk. If both are valid but differ, the primary
is selected as authoritative.
.PP
Mutating operations write the backup entry array and header before the primary
entry array and header. The protective MBR is written last, and the kernel is
asked to revalidate the device after a successful update. Existing bootstrap
code and disk-signature bytes in the first 446 bytes of the protective MBR are
preserved. A mounted or otherwise open partition makes revalidation fail with
.BR EBUSY ;
the editor checks this before writing a block device.
.PP
The migrate operation converts supported primary MBR entries to GPT entries
without changing their starting sectors, sizes, or payload sectors. It first
checks that all partitions fit between the primary and backup GPT metadata.
The old MBR bootstrap and disk signature are retained in the resulting
protective MBR. This is a partition-metadata conversion, so an external backup
is still recommended.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -p
Print all used GPT entries and identify whether the primary or backup copy was
used.
.TP
.B -c
Create an empty GPT. This destroys existing partition metadata.
.TP
.B -r
Repair GPT redundancy without changing partition entries. A valid backup is
used when the primary is invalid. Otherwise the valid primary is authoritative
and replaces an invalid or disagreeing backup. If both copies are already
consistent, no sectors are written.
.TP
.B -m
Migrate a legacy MBR containing ordinary primary partitions to GPT. Extended
and logical partitions, overlapping entries, unknown type mappings, and any
partition occupying GPT metadata sectors are rejected before writing.
.TP
.B -n
With
.BR -m ,
perform the complete migration preflight and print the proposed mappings, but
open the device read-only and write nothing.
.TP
.B -a
Add a partition. Without a partition number, the first unused GPT entry is
selected. Without a start or size, the first 1 MiB-aligned free gap is used.
The default type is the Microsoft Basic Data GUID, suitable for ordinary data
volumes including FAT.
.TP
.B -d
Delete one numbered GPT entry.
.TP
.BI -b " first-sector"
Set the first 512-byte sector explicitly.
.TP
.BI -s " sectors"
Set the partition length in 512-byte sectors.
.TP
.BI -t " type-guid"
Set a canonical textual partition type GUID.
.TP
.BI -l " label"
Set a printable ASCII label of at most 36 characters.
.SH DEVICE NODES
The GPT format contains 128 entries. The current ReBSD common disk layer
exposes GPT entries 1 through 16 as
.B sd0a
through
.BR sd0p ,
while
.B gpt -p
still validates and displays all 128 entries.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.B fdisk
continues to handle legacy MBR partition tables. Filesystems and block
transports are independent of either partition-table editor.
.PP
Migration recognizes common FAT/NTFS data types, Linux filesystem, swap, LVM
and RAID, EFI System, FreeBSD data, and Apple HFS/HFS+ types. It deliberately
refuses an MBR type for which no unambiguous GPT type is built in. Legacy BIOS
bootability is not preserved by conversion to a protective MBR.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fdisk (8),
.BR mkfs.fat (8),
.BR fsck.fat (8)
